<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Wrocław</h1><p class="st-meta">1248 CE – 1335 CE · capital Wrocław</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lower Silesian Piast duchy centred on Wrocław, whose duke Henry IV Probus sought the Polish crown before the duchy passed to Bohemia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1248 the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-the-pious">Henry II the Pious</a> divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Duchy of Silesia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-the-white">Henry III the White</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, the richest city of the region, and its surrounding lands, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a>. Wrocław had been rebuilt after the Mongol attack of 1241 and refounded under German town law.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes ruled a prosperous land of grain, cloth and trade, encouraging German settlers alongside the Polish population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus">Henry IV Probus</a>, also remembered as a minnesinger, won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> and tried to unite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> before his death in 1290. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-the-fat">Henry V the Fat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-the-good">Henry VI the Good</a> lost lands to their relatives and had to balance <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> against Poland.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-the-good">Henry VI the Good</a> recognised the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia</a> in 1327. On his death in 1335 the duchy passed directly to the Bohemian crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> remained Bohemian and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-silesia">Silesia</a> in 1742, and it has been Polish since 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-wroclaw.jpg" alt="Duchy of Wrocław" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Duchy_of_Silesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1248 CE – 1335 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wrocław</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry III the White, Henry IV Probus, Henry VI the Good</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Breslau, Duchy of Wroclaw</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1292&amp;focus=duchy-of-wroclaw" aria-label="Duchy of Wrocław on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5067 406 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Wrocław lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5387 662L5387 662M5387 662L5330 692M5387 662L5367 659M5387 662L5363 646"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5387 662h0M5330 692h0M5367 659h0M5363 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 662h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 662h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wrocław. <a href="/atlas?year=1292&amp;focus=duchy-of-wroclaw">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
